  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Isolated showers and thunderstorms into this \nevening, then a chance of showers Thursday across the Southern Tier.\n\nA secondary cold front, marked in surface observations by a wind \nshift and notable dewpoint boundary, will drift southeast across the \narea this evening. Lingering modest instability and convergence \nalong the frontal zone will support a few more isolated showers \nthrough late this evening, although the majority of the area will \nstay dry. \n\nMainly dry weather will return overnight, although abundant mid/high \nclouds will remain across much of the region. Lingering low level \nmoisture and cooling overnight will support some patchy fog across \nthe Southern Tier, Finger Lakes and North Country.\n\nA frontal wave developing over the Ohio Valley overnight will move \nacross PA Thursday and partially phase with a northern stream trough \nadvancing across Ontario and Quebec. Strengthening upper level jet \nsupport atop organized frontogenesis will support a swath of heavy \nrain across PA, but most of this will remain south of the state \nline. The northern edge of precip may bring a few light showers to \nthe Southern Tier morning through midday, with dry weather \nprevailing elsewhere. Strong upper level divergence associated with \nthe upper level jetstreak will likely support a wealth of dense \ncirrus through early afternoon before some clearing late in the day.\n\nKey Message 2...Periodic showers and scattered thunderstorms this \nweekend. \n\nDry and comfortable weather should prevail Thursday night through \nFriday as Canadian high pressure slides from the Upper Great Lakes \nto New England. Conditions will then turn more unsettled over the \nweekend as upper level troughing over Ontario Province digs \nsoutheastward and eventually closes off near the southern \nOntario/Quebec border. At the surface...an attendant elongated \nsurface low will push its warm front into our region Saturday \nafternoon and evening...followed by its trailing cold front Saturday \nnight and Sunday. Increasing moisture and modest/moderate diurnal \ninstability should support the development of some showers and \nscattered thunderstorms with the warm front Saturday afternoon and \nearly Saturday evening. Another round of more widespread showers and \na embedded storms will then likely follow Saturday night into early \nSunday as the cold front crosses our region...with cooler cyclonic \nflow/upper troughing in the wake of the front then supporting some \nadditional instability showers/storms for the balance of Sunday.",
